# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/3ce502ec8b475885b90668e617f3983cea3ae29f/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L816-L819


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The function incorrectly reverts transactions when the staking duration precisely meets the minimum staking duration requirement, due to a less than or equal comparison. This issue could lead to unnecessary reverts when services are staked for exactly the minimum required duration, potentially disrupting user transactions and affecting system usability.

## Proof of Concept
Consider a scenario where a service is staked for exactly the minimum duration required (minStakingDuration). The current check:

`if (ts <= minStakingScale && availableRewards > 0) {
    revert NotEnoughTimeStaked(serviceId, ts, minStakingScale);
}`

incorrectly triggers a revert, even though the condition of minimum duration has been met.

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Modify the conditional check to:

`if (ts < minStakingDuration && availableRewards > 0) {
    revert NotEnoughTimeStaked(serviceId, ts, minStakingDuration);
}`


## Assessed type

Invalid Validation

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/3ce502ec8b475885b90668e617f3983cea3ae29f/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L925-L935


# Vulnerability details

The function getStakingState does not define a default staking state for services that are neither evicted nor currently staked. This oversight can lead to ambiguous behavior when querying the staking state of services that have never been staked or have been unstaked without being evicted.

## Impact
This can cause confusion or incorrect assumptions in the system's operation, potentially leading to errors in interfacing systems or user interfaces that rely on a well-defined staking state for all services.

## Proof of Concept
Consider querying a service that was registered but never staked. The function will return an uninitialized staking state, which might lead to undefined behavior depending on how the state is consumed by calling contracts or interfaces.

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Add a default state, such as Unstaked, to handle cases where sInfo.tsStart is 0 and sInfo.inactivity does not exceed maxInactivityDuration. This would ensure that all paths return a valid state, clarifying the system's behavior and improving robustness.

`if (sInfo.inactivity > maxInactivityDuration) {
    stakingState = StakingState.Evicted;
} else if (sInfo.tsStart > 0) {
    stakingState = StakingState.Staked;
} else {
    stakingState = StakingState.Unstaked;  // Default state for services that are not staked or evicted
}`


