# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L398


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
During checking the ratio pass, it is commented that it must not revert, that is why it is implemented using low-level call. But, even using low-level call does not guarantee it will not revert in certain conditions.

## Proof of Concept
When `checkpoint` is called in the contract `StakingBase`, it invokes the internal function `_calculateStakingRewards`, where it invokes the internal function `_checkRatioPass` to check the ratio pass based on external activity checker implementation.
```solidity
    function checkpoint() public returns (
        uint256[] memory,
        uint256[][] memory,
        uint256[] memory,
        uint256[] memory,
        uint256[] memory evictServiceIds
    )
    {
        //....
        (uint256 lastAvailableRewards, uint256 numServices, uint256 totalRewards,
            uint256[] memory eligibleServiceIds, uint256[] memory eligibleServiceRewards,
            uint256[] memory serviceIds, uint256[][] memory serviceNonces,
            uint256[] memory serviceInactivity) = _calculateStakingRewards();
        //....
    }
```
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L603

```solidity
    function _calculateStakingRewards() internal view returns (
        uint256 lastAvailableRewards,
        uint256 numServices,
        uint256 totalRewards,
        uint256[] memory eligibleServiceIds,
        uint256[] memory eligibleServiceRewards,
        uint256[] memory serviceIds,
        uint256[][] memory serviceNonces,
        uint256[] memory serviceInactivity
    )
    {
        //....
        (ratioPass, serviceNonces[i]) = _checkRatioPass(sInfo.multisig, sInfo.nonces, ts);
        //....
    }
```
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L569

In this function, it calls the `activityChecker` address twice. Firstly, it calls the function `getMultisigNonces`, and secondly, it calls the function `isRatioPass`. Based on the comments and logic, the call to `activityChecker` must never revert. That is why a low-level call is used.
```solidity
    function _checkRatioPass(
        address multisig,
        uint256[] memory lastNonces,
        uint256 ts
    ) internal view returns (bool ratioPass, uint256[] memory currentNonces)
    {
        // Get current service multisig nonce
        // This is a low level call since it must never revert
        bytes memory activityData = abi.encodeCall(IActivityChecker.getMultisigNonces, multisig);
        (bool success, bytes memory returnData) = activityChecker.staticcall(activityData);

        // If the function call was successful, check the return value
        // The return data length must be the exact number of full slots
        if (success && returnData.length > 63 && (returnData.length % 32 == 0)) {
            // Parse nonces
            currentNonces = abi.decode(returnData, (uint256[]));

            // Get the ratio pass activity check
            activityData = abi.encodeCall(IActivityChecker.isRatioPass, (currentNonces, lastNonces, ts));
            (success, returnData) = activityChecker.staticcall(activityData);

            // The return data must match the size of bool
            if (success && returnData.length == 32) {
                ratioPass = abi.decode(returnData, (bool));
            }
        }
    }
```
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L398

Since there is not any authorization check on the implementation of the contract `activityChecker`, it can be implemented to force the function `checkpoint` to revert. So, calls using just low-level call is not enough to guarantee that it never reverts. 

 - Since the `staticcall` transfers 63/64 of remaining gas to `activityChecker`, if, for example, `activityChecker` consumes as much gas as possible, then the remaining gas in `StakingBase` will not be enough to proceed with `checkpoint` function and it will lead to out-of-gas error in `StakingBase`. In other words, `activityChecker` consumes all the forwarded gas, so only 1/64 of remaining gas will be available for execution of the rest of the code in `StakingBase` which is not enough, so leading to out-of-gas revert. For better understanding, the following implementation of `getMultisigNonces` will lead to a situation that all the gas left after the invocation of `getMultisigNonces` in `StakingBase` is not enough for the execution of the rest of the code, leading to revert.
 ```solidity
     function getMultisigNonces(address multisig) external view virtual returns (uint256[] memory nonces) {
        nonces = new uint256[](1);
        nonces[0] = IMultisig(multisig).nonce();
        uint256 i;
        while(gasleft() > 100){
            i++;
        }
    }
 ```
 - Moreover, even if the call to `activityChecker` is successful, and there is enough gas left afterward, it is not still guaranteed that it does not lead to revert. Because, if `activityChecker` returns a data that is not decodable to `uint256[]`, then it will revert here:
 https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L413
 Suppose `activityChecker` is implemented as follows:
  ```solidity
     function getMultisigNonces(address multisig) external view virtual returns (uint256[] memory nonces) {
        nonces = new uint256[](1);
        nonces[0] = IMultisig(multisig).nonce();
        assembly{
            mstore(0, 0x20)
            mstore(0x20, 0x02)
            mstore(0x40, 0x01)
            return(0, 0x60)
        }
    }
 ```
 - Note that the `multisig` is assumed to be implemented as expected without any issue as its address is enforced to correspond to the authorized multisig proxy bytecode hash during staking a service.
 https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/registries/contracts/staking/StakingBase.sol#L761

 In summary, it is not clear from the code why a low-level call is used when checking ratio pass `_checkRatioPass`.
  - If it must not revert, then `activityChecker` can be implemented such that it forces out-of-gas error or wrong return data, all leading to revert.
  - If `activityChecker` is guaranteed to be implemented such that it does not lead to revert, then the comment "This is a low level call since it must never revert" is misleading.

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

It is recommended to limit the amount of gas forwarded to `activityChecker` as well as using try-catch during decoding the returned data to be on the safe side even if the returned data is encoded wrongly.




