# Vulnerability details

## Impact
An attacker can force the protocol to distribute staking incentives to staking instances two times by engineering the staking instances and misusing the replayability of bridging protocols.

## Proof of Concept

The attack scenario is as follows:

 - The attacker creates two staking instances (instanceA and instanceB) by calling the function `createStakingInstance`. Note that both are valid and honest contracts, so the malicious intention of the attacker is not recognizable. Morevoer, the attacker creates these two instances with two different addresses, so that the `deployer` of these instances will be different from each other.
 ```solidity
     function createStakingInstance(
        address implementation,
        bytes memory initPayload
    ) external returns (address payable instance) {
        //....

        bytes memory deploymentData = abi.encodePacked(type(StakingProxy).creationCode,
            uint256(uint160(implementation)));

        // solhint-disable-next-line no-inline-assembly
        assembly {
            instance := create2(0x0, add(0x20, deploymentData), mload(deploymentData), salt)
        }

        //....
    }
 ```
 https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/registries/contracts/staking/StakingFactory.sol#L168

  - The implementation of instanceA is similar exactly to `StakingToken` contract. But the instanceB is a customized version of `StakingToken`. The only difference of instanceB from the instanceA is that the attacker is able to pause the depositing mechanism in the instanceB. So, the following modification is implemented in instanceB:
  ```solidity

    address public immutable owner = address(1); // The address of the attacker

    function pause(bool _enabled) public{
        require(msg.sender == owner, "only owner is allowed to pause!");
        paused = _enabled;
    }

    function deposit(uint256 amount) external {
        require(!paused, "it is paused, so depositing is not allowed.");

        //...
    }
  ```
  https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/registries/contracts/staking/StakingToken.sol#L115

  - Both instances work properly and honestly. Until the time that staking incentives are claimed on L1, and they are supposed to be distributed to these contracts on L2. Suppose 100e18 incentives are going to be distributed on the target chain to those instances (90e18 to instanceA and 10e18 to instanceB). This can be triggered by calling the function `claimStakingIncentivesBatch` on L1.
  https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-olas/blob/main/tokenomics/contracts/Dispenser.sol#L1058

  - Before the message is received on L2, the attacker calls the function `pause(true)` on instanceB.
  - When the `receiveMessage` on L2 is called, the bridged data will be processed.
